ithub.com/users/krassowski/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krassowski","id":5832902,"login":"krassowski","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU4MzI5MDI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/738dbb182c197f545e49ad9ca07e563e313017f1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-15T08:59:55Z","email":"5832902+krassowski@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Michał Krassowski"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-15T08:59:55Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Fix memory leaks affecting common everyday use scenarios (#19370)\n\n## References\n\n- Builds on top of #19318 which is follow up to #19307 that introduced\nthe signal lifetime lint rules.\n- Fixes #19267: the collapse-state listeners are now registered once per\npanel, guarded by a `WeakSet` as suggested there, and cleaned up through\nthe panel''s disposal.\n\nThe fixes were found by tracing retaining paths in heap snapshots for\nthree scenarios: deleting cells, closing a notebook, and closing one of\nseveral views of a notebook.\n\n> Note to reviewer: since this PR builds on top of #19318, until that is\nmerged and this PR rebased you may want to focus on\n6125aa1285b7a56597b072ed02f915f89570abb7 alone\n\n## Code changes\n\n### 1. Moving or deleting cells\n\nCreating, moving and deleting cells (every cell move disposes the old\nwidget and creates a new one):\n\n- The vendored y-codemirror undo plugin deletes its selection metadata\nfrom the undo manager stacks on destroy. The stack items live as long as\nthe document and the metadata pinned the destroyed editor view, and\nthrough it the whole deleted cell. Its only purpose is restoring the\nselection, which has no meaning once the view is gone.\n- `Cell`, `CodeCell` and `InputArea` dispose the widgets that can be\nswapped out of their layout (input and output areas with their\nplaceholders, and the rendered widget of a markdown cell in edit mode):\n`Widget.dispose()` only disposes layout children, and an undisposed\noutput area stays connected to the output model.\n- `OutputArea` removes its prompt overlay click listener and\n`ResizeHandle` removes its DOM listeners on dispose, so DOM retained by\nanything else no longer keeps the widget graphs alive.\n\n### 2. Closing a notebook\n\nClosing a notebook, console or file editor (with or without debugging\never used):\n\n- `StaticNotebook.dispose` disposes all cell widgets explicitly. In the\n`full` and `defer` windowing modes out-of-viewport cells are not layout\nchildren, so `Widget.dispose()` misses them and their connections to the\nshared model leak every one of them; in the default `contentVisibility`\nmode this is a safety net (verified by ablation: the probes, which run\nunder the default mode, pass without it).\n- `ReactWidget` unmounts its React root on dispose. It only unmounted on\ndetach, so any React-based item disposed while already detached (such as\ntoolbar buttons parked in a disposed cell) kept its root and every\nsubscription of the rendered components, including `UseSignal`\nconnections to the `CommandRegistry`.\n- `DebuggerHandler` connects its per-widget handlers with the widget as\nreceiver and releases them on widget disposal:\n`Signal.clearData(widget)` removes the connections and the disposal hook\ndeletes the id-keyed map entries. Previously those maps on this\napplication-lifetime object kept a closure over every closed widget\nforever whenever debugging had not been started for it. The handlers\nstay connected for the widget''s lifetime, including through the\nnon-debugging passes of `updateWidget` (covered by a new regression\ntest). Also guards `update`, `updateContext`, `updateWidget` and\n`createHandler` against widgets disposed during awaits, and clears\n`activeWidget` on disposal.\n- `DocumentConnectionManager` sweeps disposed virtual documents out of\nits `documents` map: the adapter clears its `virtualDocument` reference\nbefore the disposal handler runs, so the existing `documents.delete`\ncall never fired. `VirtualDocument.dispose` also clears its editor maps,\nand the `adapters` entry is removed under the document''s current path so\na renamed document does not survive its closure.\n- `CellBarExtension` drops its reference to the last tracker when its\npanel is disposed.\n\n### 3. Closing a second view of a notebook\n\nClosing one of several views of a document (New View for Notebook,\ncloned output views):\n\n- `ExecutionIndicator` disposes its model in a `dispose` override\n(covering the toolbar item and the status bar item alike), the model''s\nkernel and cleanup handlers connect with the model as receiver so\ndisposal clears them, and the elapsed-time interval terminates itself\nwhen the model is disposed while a kernel is busy.\n- The notebook extension connects its restore-data, collapse-state and\ncompleter handlers with the panel as receiver, and makes the\ncollapse-state and completer connections once per panel instead of on\nevery activation or settings change.\n- The notebook output log connections in `nboutput.ts` use the panel as\nreceiver.\n- `DocumentConnectionManager.registerAdapter` disconnects its\n`pathChanged` slot when the adapter is disposed.\n- `StaticNotebook.dispose` disposes the view model it creates, which\ndisconnects it from the shared cell list. `WindowedList` does not take\nownership of the caller-provided model, so its lifetime stays with the\ncreator.\n\n### Dependencies and configuration\n\n- `@jupyter/web-components` and `@jupyter/react-components` are bumped\nfrom 0.16.6 to 0.17.1. In 0.16.x, `Toolbar.connectedCallback` bound\n`fillColor` against its parent element, making fast-foundation create a\npermanent `DesignTokenNode` for that element\n(jupyterlab-contrib/jupyter-ui-toolkit#126 removed this in 0.17.1).\nEvery cell the user activated, and every closed view, leaked its whole\ndetached DOM tree through these nodes, re-anchoring widget graphs\nthrough any DOM listener. The 0.17.1 toolbar no longer synchronizes its\nfill color, which is the visual change that PR describes as aligning\nwith Jupyter styling.\n- `eslint.config.mjs` adds the kernel session types to `longLivedTypes`\nfor the signal lifetime rules (the option replaces the default list, so\nthe twelve defaults are restated). This yields no findings on the\ncurrent codebase, including on the sites fixed here, which other gates\nof the rules skip. The leaks in this PR were found with runtime heap\nsnapshots, not lint; the config change guards new code connecting to\nsession signals.\n\n## Measurements\n\nAll scenarios use a 75-cell notebook.\n\nScenario 1: add 75 cells each printing 256 KiB, make each the active\ncell, run them, then delete them all. Moving a cell (keyboard or drag\nand drop) disposes and recreates its widget, so cell moves exercise the\nsame path per move.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR   |\n| ------------------ | --------- | --------- |\n| `CodeCell`         | +75       | 0         |\n| `OutputArea`       | +75       | 0         |\n| `OutputAreaModel`  | +75       | 0         |\n| heap used          | +77.2 MiB | +35.9 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +67,992   | +50,602   |\n| JS event listeners | +205,329  | +6,345    |\n\nScenario 2, closing a notebook: open and close a saved 75 cell notebook\n5 times, without interacting with it beyond letting it render and its\nkernel start.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR  |\n| ------------------ | --------- | -------- |\n| `NotebookPanel`    | +5        | 0        |\n| `Notebook`         | +5        | 0        |\n| `CodeCell`         | +380      | 0        |\n| heap used          | +14.3 MiB | +6.0 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +9,916    | +5,716   |\n| JS event listeners | +63,548   | +725     |\n\nEvery closed notebook used to be retained whole with all of its cells.\n\nScenario 3: open a second view with New View for Notebook\n(`docmanager:clone`), let it render, close it, 5 times.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR  |\n| ------------------ | --------- | -------- |\n| `Notebook`         | +5        | 0        |\n| `CodeCell`         | +375      | 0        |\n| `OutputArea`       | +375      | 0        |\n| `CodeMirrorEditor` | 0         | 0        |\n| heap used          | +12.5 MiB | +3.0 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +13,950   | +5,619   |\n| JS event listeners | +114,645  | +580     |\n\nNote the listeners row: tens of thousands of leaked listeners per\nscenario drop to a few hundred.\n\n> Taken with temporary Galata probes, not included here: instance counts\nthrough `Runtime.queryObjects` after a snapshot-forced GC, heap from\n`Runtime.getHeapUsage`, DOM and listeners from `Performance.getMetrics`.\nhttps://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/issues/19362 discusses making\nthat a part of CI.\n\n## Remaining known growth\n\n- Deleting cells keeps their content, including outputs, in the yjs\ndocument history so the deletion can be undone; that is the bulk of the\nremaining heap growth in scenario 1 and is intended.\n- Output rendering that is still in flight when a view is closed is\nparked forever by the async render pipeline: the pending render\ngenerator holds the output area and its host DOM. This is bounded by how\nmuch rendering was pending at close (the probes wait for rendering to\nfinish before closing, as a user looking at the view would). Worth a\nfollow-up issue: async output rendering should abort on dispose.\n- The rest of the per-cycle heap and DOM growth in scenarios 2 and 3 is\nsession-scoped state (kernel and session bookkeeping, notifications),\nnot widget retention.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nLower memory use for everyday notebook work: creating, moving and\ndeleting cells no longer accumulates dead cells and editors, closing\nnotebooks no longer retains them whole, and closing extra views of a\ndocument no longer retains the whole view.\n\nThe toolkit bump also changes the toolbar to no longer stand out from\nits parent background, aligning with Jupyter styling.\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **YES**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES)\n- AI tools and models used: Fable 5","tree":{"sha":"9a91275c6036ffc92548618fcc918a3390f4f914","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/trees/9a91275c6036ffc92548618fcc918a3390f4f914"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/commits/738dbb182c197f545e49ad9ca07e563e313017f1","verification":{"payload":"tree 9a91275c6036ffc92548618fcc918a3390f4f914\nparent 09234a77b5d5e10b75c03413ae23e254d48bc85e\nauthor Michał Krassowski <5832902+krassowski@users.noreply.github.com> 1786784395 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786784395 +0100\n\nFix memory leaks affecting common everyday use scenarios (#19370)\n\n## References\n\n- Builds on top of #19318 which is follow up to #19307 that introduced\nthe signal lifetime lint rules.\n- Fixes #19267: the collapse-state listeners are now registered once per\npanel, guarded by a `WeakSet` as suggested there, and cleaned up through\nthe panel''s disposal.\n\nThe fixes were found by tracing retaining paths in heap snapshots for\nthree scenarios: deleting cells, closing a notebook, and closing one of\nseveral views of a notebook.\n\n> Note to reviewer: since this PR builds on top of #19318, until that is\nmerged and this PR rebased you may want to focus on\n6125aa1285b7a56597b072ed02f915f89570abb7 alone\n\n## Code changes\n\n### 1. Moving or deleting cells\n\nCreating, moving and deleting cells (every cell move disposes the old\nwidget and creates a new one):\n\n- The vendored y-codemirror undo plugin deletes its selection metadata\nfrom the undo manager stacks on destroy. The stack items live as long as\nthe document and the metadata pinned the destroyed editor view, and\nthrough it the whole deleted cell. Its only purpose is restoring the\nselection, which has no meaning once the view is gone.\n- `Cell`, `CodeCell` and `InputArea` dispose the widgets that can be\nswapped out of their layout (input and output areas with their\nplaceholders, and the rendered widget of a markdown cell in edit mode):\n`Widget.dispose()` only disposes layout children, and an undisposed\noutput area stays connected to the output model.\n- `OutputArea` removes its prompt overlay click listener and\n`ResizeHandle` removes its DOM listeners on dispose, so DOM retained by\nanything else no longer keeps the widget graphs alive.\n\n### 2. Closing a notebook\n\nClosing a notebook, console or file editor (with or without debugging\never used):\n\n- `StaticNotebook.dispose` disposes all cell widgets explicitly. In the\n`full` and `defer` windowing modes out-of-viewport cells are not layout\nchildren, so `Widget.dispose()` misses them and their connections to the\nshared model leak every one of them; in the default `contentVisibility`\nmode this is a safety net (verified by ablation: the probes, which run\nunder the default mode, pass without it).\n- `ReactWidget` unmounts its React root on dispose. It only unmounted on\ndetach, so any React-based item disposed while already detached (such as\ntoolbar buttons parked in a disposed cell) kept its root and every\nsubscription of the rendered components, including `UseSignal`\nconnections to the `CommandRegistry`.\n- `DebuggerHandler` connects its per-widget handlers with the widget as\nreceiver and releases them on widget disposal:\n`Signal.clearData(widget)` removes the connections and the disposal hook\ndeletes the id-keyed map entries. Previously those maps on this\napplication-lifetime object kept a closure over every closed widget\nforever whenever debugging had not been started for it. The handlers\nstay connected for the widget''s lifetime, including through the\nnon-debugging passes of `updateWidget` (covered by a new regression\ntest). Also guards `update`, `updateContext`, `updateWidget` and\n`createHandler` against widgets disposed during awaits, and clears\n`activeWidget` on disposal.\n- `DocumentConnectionManager` sweeps disposed virtual documents out of\nits `documents` map: the adapter clears its `virtualDocument` reference\nbefore the disposal handler runs, so the existing `documents.delete`\ncall never fired. `VirtualDocument.dispose` also clears its editor maps,\nand the `adapters` entry is removed under the document''s current path so\na renamed document does not survive its closure.\n- `CellBarExtension` drops its reference to the last tracker when its\npanel is disposed.\n\n### 3. Closing a second view of a notebook\n\nClosing one of several views of a document (New View for Notebook,\ncloned output views):\n\n- `ExecutionIndicator` disposes its model in a `dispose` override\n(covering the toolbar item and the status bar item alike), the model''s\nkernel and cleanup handlers connect with the model as receiver so\ndisposal clears them, and the elapsed-time interval terminates itself\nwhen the model is disposed while a kernel is busy.\n- The notebook extension connects its restore-data, collapse-state and\ncompleter handlers with the panel as receiver, and makes the\ncollapse-state and completer connections once per panel instead of on\nevery activation or settings change.\n- The notebook output log connections in `nboutput.ts` use the panel as\nreceiver.\n- `DocumentConnectionManager.registerAdapter` disconnects its\n`pathChanged` slot when the adapter is disposed.\n- `StaticNotebook.dispose` disposes the view model it creates, which\ndisconnects it from the shared cell list. `WindowedList` does not take\nownership of the caller-provided model, so its lifetime stays with the\ncreator.\n\n### Dependencies and configuration\n\n- `@jupyter/web-components` and `@jupyter/react-components` are bumped\nfrom 0.16.6 to 0.17.1. In 0.16.x, `Toolbar.connectedCallback` bound\n`fillColor` against its parent element, making fast-foundation create a\npermanent `DesignTokenNode` for that element\n(jupyterlab-contrib/jupyter-ui-toolkit#126 removed this in 0.17.1).\nEvery cell the user activated, and every closed view, leaked its whole\ndetached DOM tree through these nodes, re-anchoring widget graphs\nthrough any DOM listener. The 0.17.1 toolbar no longer synchronizes its\nfill color, which is the visual change that PR describes as aligning\nwith Jupyter styling.\n- `eslint.config.mjs` adds the kernel session types to `longLivedTypes`\nfor the signal lifetime rules (the option replaces the default list, so\nthe twelve defaults are restated). This yields no findings on the\ncurrent codebase, including on the sites fixed here, which other gates\nof the rules skip. The leaks in this PR were found with runtime heap\nsnapshots, not lint; the config change guards new code connecting to\nsession signals.\n\n## Measurements\n\nAll scenarios use a 75-cell notebook.\n\nScenario 1: add 75 cells each printing 256 KiB, make each the active\ncell, run them, then delete them all. Moving a cell (keyboard or drag\nand drop) disposes and recreates its widget, so cell moves exercise the\nsame path per move.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR   |\n| ------------------ | --------- | --------- |\n| `CodeCell`         | +75       | 0         |\n| `OutputArea`       | +75       | 0         |\n| `OutputAreaModel`  | +75       | 0         |\n| heap used          | +77.2 MiB | +35.9 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +67,992   | +50,602   |\n| JS event listeners | +205,329  | +6,345    |\n\nScenario 2, closing a notebook: open and close a saved 75 cell notebook\n5 times, without interacting with it beyond letting it render and its\nkernel start.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR  |\n| ------------------ | --------- | -------- |\n| `NotebookPanel`    | +5        | 0        |\n| `Notebook`         | +5        | 0        |\n| `CodeCell`         | +380      | 0        |\n| heap used          | +14.3 MiB | +6.0 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +9,916    | +5,716   |\n| JS event listeners | +63,548   | +725     |\n\nEvery closed notebook used to be retained whole with all of its cells.\n\nScenario 3: open a second view with New View for Notebook\n(`docmanager:clone`), let it render, close it, 5 times.\n\n|                    | `main`    | this PR  |\n| ------------------ | --------- | -------- |\n| `Notebook`         | +5        | 0        |\n| `CodeCell`         | +375      | 0        |\n| `OutputArea`       | +375      | 0        |\n| `CodeMirrorEditor` | 0         | 0        |\n| heap used          | +12.5 MiB | +3.0 MiB |\n| DOM nodes          | +13,950   | +5,619   |\n| JS event listeners | +114,645  | +580     |\n\nNote the listeners row: tens of thousands of leaked listeners per\nscenario drop to a few hundred.\n\n> Taken with temporary Galata probes, not included here: instance counts\nthrough `Runtime.queryObjects` after a snapshot-forced GC, heap from\n`Runtime.getHeapUsage`, DOM and listeners from `Performance.getMetrics`.\nhttps://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/issues/19362 discusses making\nthat a part of CI.\n\n## Remaining known growth\n\n- Deleting cells keeps their content, including outputs, in the yjs\ndocument history so the deletion can be undone; that is the bulk of the\nremaining heap growth in scenario 1 and is intended.\n- Output rendering that is still in flight when a view is closed is\nparked forever by the async render pipeline: the pending render\ngenerator holds the output area and its host DOM. This is bounded by how\nmuch rendering was pending at close (the probes wait for rendering to\nfinish before closing, as a user looking at the view would). Worth a\nfollow-up issue: async output rendering should abort on dispose.\n- The rest of the per-cycle heap and DOM growth in scenarios 2 and 3 is\nsession-scoped state (kernel and session bookkeeping, notifications),\nnot widget retention.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nLower memory use for everyday notebook work: creating, moving and\ndeleting cells no longer accumulates dead cells and editors, closing\nnotebooks no longer retains them whole, and closing extra views of a\ndocument no longer retains the whole view.\n\nThe toolkit bump also changes the toolbar to no longer stand out from\nits parent background, aligning with Jupyter styling.\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **YES**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES)\n- AI tools and models used: Fable 5","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqgCqLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAikIQAAlaZVBYTcoZ6j7mC3zIrfp+\nsz1g9YnLj7AjpXowiuUWCwEfDENvPXx2cAL3uky4s0/Geyqzu7bZtszHeUd4vf2P\nwsy5z+RdQ27H+rvmgPRJ9y3Chg40akHM89Lgc2m9ARZ/C8b1HBzTmOe75IZq9sQ9\no3w0N2RH+vy+CAh9v0Fgs9Vq5/+wJr1o0hxNYkCckbT2ZjmrhYIodkG8BcoR06j7\nzo+y20j55ZrI2DIEn/J3BVfYZ2le8HErqB/LiYkEvGZv8JLe4SqCpFOm71cXBgZA\niH/BIZXsIvV7cWohTpxRpYxJokQwgVaGQmYViKGGlqaIq9Goq8amDRMP22z+HDNe\nSDHOjEWMoHBvcLC/Gop/stTIYYlqLn7gsT2eiHRAQ0NlgZzF6egHXgMgGaRtV+JR\nzvSr0seMAnw/WzOK/7di6o5PS2dB0o0yqAIraw80cG2QKobOwfEWEGeXLcorEgSR\nZPwiei8iO+lWUa8Gb3E6tSZXOpVN0qdJXH9ZTmHzNvktq9R4TQuVQW1EnsHwGx48\nVJwdQC6cDocsVS8ZPtkupXP7c6QOpyG