moved item in the\nreactive toolbar.\nThat way the `widgetPositions` map is always up to date.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nNone\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **YES**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES)\n- AI tools and models used: claude code, Sonnet 4.6\n\n## Additional context\n\nThis PR could be backported to previous branches (until 4.1.x)","tree":{"sha":"a96134e5ffbdc19a66dd3a642d68408949e7cabc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/trees/a96134e5ffbdc19a66dd3a642d68408949e7cabc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/commits/10495e8e976f073fc765ddd5ef517d5a51c594ab","verification":{"payload":"tree a96134e5ffbdc19a66dd3a642d68408949e7cabc\nparent 7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b\nauthor Nicolas Brichet <32258950+brichet@users.noreply.github.com> 1786176802 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786176802 +0100\n\nUpdate the item positions when an item is removed from reactive toolbar (#19227)\n\n## References\n\nFixes #19226\n\n## Code changes\n\nUpdate the position of all items following a removed item in the\nreactive toolbar.\nThat way the `widgetPositions` map is always up to date.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nNone\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **YES**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES)\n- AI tools and models used: claude code, Sonnet 4.6\n\n## Additional context\n\nThis PR could be backported to previous branches (until 4.1.x)","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqduUiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+3gQAAnkRnuK7G2WOW8wcUE2f6wj\nxW7SMUlbuOzb5FmGwSs2Oxk7ljU4KEJjYtShr5vIOydlBLBV/6tDWnHtTDp2geYW\n18+skiYIMUDENvqW0X4936JPyJeTXvn5lmyHcTstL5fYCVoRXnxDp07wbiS3i5h9\nhRl+DdSc7s24dYrEL3NLVI1/acqvE6lc3K1ImSBh8ZFAyH+R7AM/ZMsJoMEoYPCf\nxP6GrwAbEbmxvb8U6EgpmWqGYWNonnKp4dE+vyte3tj61JOeFJ0np1C/UciELcAJ\nLgoNWWlgQcbzzNNeBDWW+KheSUmPRVHf8EkcIUp7CX3PhuevwecFiJnLEBxPuqrR\nEh2ggAw3jQlYoeEiwO0zy3GOu3esFjnRvaV7TNjK6asTY7UP0TEQLKKubPIItZtL\nRq4aCXRRtALI0n+/yu22gHmHxp8qQPMpN8B+iPfad+qBlHurAYB8r3a1sMZivAWI\nHCFaxyhrKaQlBWujkAC6USOXaWtyUBcfUElrHR4ZjA3DWTXUO6n8i7/n+cEky23N\nfn3HPfWZsL821IN/jwzqEayUizqHFLjAjJDpz3CslqqBtBKZoJ/vPOMQLom8Gm+h\nfnJuIz3yO5UbM+vvKqz1zst8M1Ll55X6l8HC1N9+I/YDfARKLYkGffyUPf+jL1N9\nHLNyjE/eKvxZT+zHOr4O\n=paHA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-08T08:13:24Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commit/10495e8e976f073fc765ddd5ef517d5a51c594ab","node_id":"C_kwDOA5k_I9oAKDEwNDk1ZThlOTc2ZjA3M2ZjNzY1ZGRkNWVmNTE3ZDVhNTFjNTk0YWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commit/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b","sha":"7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b"}],"sha":"10495e8e976f073fc765ddd5ef517d5a51c594ab","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/10495e8e976f073fc765ddd5ef517d5a51c594ab"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5832902?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/krassowski","id":5832902,"login":"krassowski","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU4MzI5MDI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/krassowski","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-07T17:11:25Z","email":"5832902+krassowski@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Michał Krassowski"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-07T17:11:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Speed up large and streamed text outputs with incremental autolinking, preserve selection (#17197)\n\n## References\n\nFixes #16957\n\n## Code changes\n\nMakes the auto-linking logic execute asynchronously, at the speed\nmatching the approximate refresh rate of standard monitors. Appends new\nnodes in the textual streams without removing the existing ones\nminimising layout computation cost.\n\n1. **Spreads the work across frames**\n- Rendering happens in small steps inside a `requestAnimationFrame` loop\n(a browser callback that runs about once per screen refresh). Each frame\ndoes at most ~10 ms of auto-linking and then continues on the next\nframe, so one output cannot freeze the page.\n- Each step processes a block of text of about 8 KB (extended to the\nnext space), rather than one word or line at a time, which lowers the\nper-step overhead.\n- When several large outputs render at once, they take turns: only one\noutput is worked on per frame, and the loop cycles through them so each\nkeeps making visible progress and none stays blank.\n- A safety timer (named a \"watchdog\" in the code) still shows the plain\ntext if the browser stops sending animation frames, for example in a\nbackground tab or under heavy load. It only acts when frames have\ngenuinely stopped, not when an output is simply waiting for its turn.\n2. **Prioritises what the user can see**\n- Outputs scrolled off screen are found with an `IntersectionObserver`.\nWe use it instead of measuring visibility ourselves, because measuring\ndirectly would force the browser to recompute layout at a bad moment.\n- Off-screen outputs keep rendering, but quietly, during the browser''s\nidle time (`requestIdleCallback`, with a timer-based fallback for\nSafari). They are then ready when scrolled to, without slowing down the\noutputs on screen.\n- As soon as an off-screen output scrolls back into view, it returns to\nnormal per-frame rendering. The first paint of any output is never\ndelayed by visibility.\n3. **Updates the DOM without rebuilding it** (append-only, no trashing)\n   - Each output keeps a live `<pre>` element in two parts:\n- a \"committed\" part at the start: text that has already been linked and\nis never touched again;\n- a \"tail\" at the end: the not-yet-linked text, shown as plain text\nimmediately (with terminal colours kept), split into small pieces.\n- Every frame moves a little of the tail into the committed part after\nlinking it, so the amount of page update per frame depends only on how\nmuch was processed that frame, not on the total output size.\nFor streamed output, the next chunk reuses the page content from the\nprevious render instead of rebuilding it. Only the small region at the\nend that could still change is redone, for example a link split across\ntwo chunks (chunk one ends with `www.`, chunk two starts with\n`example.com`). This replaces the earlier approach that compared and\nswapped nodes.\n4. **Adds a logic to retain selection when updating text**. This is\nrequired because when we are replacing `www.example.com` in partially\nrendered text output `aaa www.example.com ccc` we are also implicitly\ndestroying the selection. This also leads to an improvement to status\nquo where selection was being destroyed on each stream chunk being\nadded.\n\n#### Notable edge cases\n\n- Search highlighting keeps working. Because the committed part is never\nrewritten, highlights added to it stay in place across frames. If\nanother feature changes the tail (for example search splitting or\nwrapping the text there), only the tail is rebuilt so committed\nhighlights are kept, and a full rebuild is used only as a last resort.\n- Text selection is preserved. When text is replaced during linking, the\nselection is remembered as character positions and restored afterwards.\nThis also fixes an existing problem where the selection was lost every\ntime a new chunk of streamed text arrived.\n- File-path links in error outputs (such as File \"example.py\", line 12)\nkeep their line number. Error outputs link both URLs and file paths, and\na file path can contain spaces, so steps there split on line breaks\ninstead of on spaces.\n- For very large outputs the slow part is the browser re-laying-out the\nshifted text on each frame, so the total time depends mostly on how many\nframes are used. In that case the per-frame budget is allowed to grow\n(up to 200 ms) so the work finishes in fewer frames, while small outputs\nkeep the responsive 10 ms budget. The budget only grows from this\noutput''s own previous frame, so several outputs rendering together do\nnot push each other''s budgets up.\n\n#### Code organisation\n\n- The live-page logic, node merging, and selection helpers are now\ncontained in the `LiveText` class in `rendermime/src/livetext.ts`.\n`renderers.ts` keeps the frame scheduling and the auto-linking.\n- A benchmark and analysis scripts were added under\n`galata/test/benchmark` to measure both stdout and stderr, streaming\nversus re-opening a notebook, and scaling with output size and number of\noutputs.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\n### It''s faster\n\nThe output content is displayed when it gets returned from the kernel as\nsoon as possible - no more choking the rendering pipeline due to too\nmuch output streamed at once.\n\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a8122a4e-4ea2-4ca4-ba35-53d5990b0611\n\nAs an example, when profiling the first paint of full (non-linkified)\ntext occurred in under 4 seconds (allowing me to read the last line),\nwhereas on 4.4.0a2 I had to wait 29 seconds to get it (but it was\nalready with all links displayed).\n\n### The links show up with a delay\n\nThe links are displayed as soon as these are processed. This means that\nusers may now see non-clickable links, but as seen on the recording\nabove, even though initially the text is not \"linkified\" the last links\nshows up faster than before! This is not guaranteed - it will depend on\nthe size of the output.\n\n> Note: the recording is from an earlier iteration; now links only show\nup with delay on much larger outputs; keeping that older recording as it\ndemonstrates the idea well; see benchmarks below for when it kicks in\nnowadays.\n\n### Selection is fun again\n\nSelection of outputs that is being streamed is not destroyed when new\noutput arrives\n\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/97741eb8-c6f0-4ba7-b11d-61253a617311\n\n### There is an opt-out\n\nI can''t think of a way this could introduce a regression, but still just\nin case if someone was depending on some quirk of the previous\nimplementation, this PR includes a setting to disable incremental\nrendering.\n\n<img width=\"975\" height=\"299\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/be35a0eb-a1bb-4626-89a2-9f00c410288e\"\n/>\n\nQuestion: should we use \"auto-link\" or \"autolink\"?\n\n### Latest benchmarks (comitted)\n\n### Table 1 Streaming on cell execution\n\n\n- incremental = this PR\n- synchronous = before/with incremental auto linking turned off\n\n\nDuring interactive execution both: full text, and all links show up much\nfaster than before. Note that in synchronous case all-text = all-links.\n\n\n#### Streaming stdout (web links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>1a - Streaming · stdout (web links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>85</td><td>79-91</td><td rowspan=\"3\">50</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">17</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>448</td><td>444-454</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>448</td><td>444-454</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>91</td><td>89-93</td><td rowspan=\"3\">117</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">100</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>1,819</td><td>1,814-1,830</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>1,819</td><td>1,814-1,830</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n\n#### Streaming stderr (web + path links)\n\n\n<table>\n  <caption>1b - Streaming · stderr (web + path links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>134</td><td>108-169</td><td rowspan=\"3\">55</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">22</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>452</td><td>414-476</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>452</td><td>414-476</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>284</td><td>246-318</td><td rowspan=\"3\">183</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">117</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>12,283</td><td>12,132-12,409</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>12,283</td><td>12,132-12,409</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n\n### Table 2 Rendering on re-opening a notebook\n\n#### Bulk rendering stdout (web links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>2a - Re-open · stdout (web links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td><td rowspan=\"3\">72</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">17</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td><td rowspan=\"3\">90</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">28</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n#### Bulk rendering on re-open · stderr (web + path links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>2b - Re-open · stderr (web + path links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td><td rowspan=\"3\">95</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">22</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td><td rowspan=\"3\">95</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">33</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\nNote that for synchronous scenario all values are identical on reopening\nhere as expected.\n\n#### Scaling with output size - streaming stderr\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1772a31e-55d0-421b-afab-f68be7baf759\"\n/>\n\n(1600 blocks synchronous pipeline did not run until completion as it\nexceeded 5 minutes threshold)\n\n#### Scaling with output size - re-opening a notebook with stdout\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/66fbcb83-f655-4e01-8958-a057b7786a40\"\n/>\n\n#### Scaling with number of outputs - re-opening a notebook with stderr\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/470b1ea5-87c0-4721-99b8-adeb3c179ecf\"\n/>\n\n#### Linkification only starts to lag behind text when re-opening a\nnotebook with very large output\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9b6c5dff-82d0-42a8-9150-98a65860c40e\"\n/>\n\n\n\n## Future work\n\nPotential improvements:\n- We could try to infer the refresh rate of the monitor using intervals\nbetween `requestAnimationFrame` and use it instead of a hard-coded time\nlimit; this might improve the experience for users with high-refresh\nrate monitors (bot only if they are also using high-end CPU/GPU\ncombination that can process enough `linkify` passes in such shorter\nduration of the frame to avoid frame dropping)\n- We could avoid the layout cost by imposing size restriction on the\noutput if no new text was streamed (but it is only being auto-linked).\nThis is because the links should be rendered using the same font (but\nonly different colour).\n- I attempted it but in initial attempts I did not see significant gains\n- Instead of constantly calling `requestAnimationFrame` (and cancelling\nit) we could call it once and update arguments if new chunk is streamed;\nthis is because there is an overhead associated (approx 0.6s for each\n10s so not terrible, but noticeable); instead we could pass state via a\n`WeakMap` or similar.\n\n\n\n### Already handled in this PR (were previously listed here)\n\n- Re-layout cost: with the append-only update, committed text is never\nlaid out again. Together with `contain: style layout` this gives a very\ngood performance; I previously explored also fixing the size but it is\nnot necessary nor desirable\n- Frame timing on capable machines: handled by letting the per-frame\nbudget grow, without having to detect the exact monitor refresh rate.\n\n### To consider in future PRs\n\n- **Parsing repeats over the whole output on every chunk**: Each\nstreamed chunk re-cleans and re-parses all the text received so far, not\nonly the new part. Now that updating the page is cheap per chunk, this\nparsing is the main remaining cost for long streams. Parsing only the\nnew text (while carrying terminal-colour state from the previous chunk)\nwould fix it.\n- **High-refresh-rate monitors**: We could measure the time between\nframes and stop assuming 60 frames per second, but this only helps where\nthe CPU and GPU can also fit enough linking work into the shorter frame.\nI did attempt that but it did not seem worth it at the time.\n- **Animation-frame scheduling overhead**: The loop requests and cancels\na frame on every step (a small but measurable cost). Requesting a frame\nonce and passing state through a `WeakMap` would remove it (at a cost of\nmaking it harder to reason about).\n- **Progress bars that rewrite the same line** (using `\\r`, like tqdm):\nThese still redo work on each update. This is acceptable for now because\nwe do not expect outputs which are 10s of MBs in size to be constrained\nto a single line (which would be somewhat pathological, even for random\nbytes to not include a single `\\n`), but the impact on when it does\nhappen could be limited with more work.\n- **One `IntersectionObserver` is created per output**: A single shared\nobserver would be lighter for notebooks with thousands of outputs (but\npotentially make the code more complex to reason about).\n- **Using document fragments for one-pass insertion**: This could reduce\nthe number of DOM insertions (but make it larger); in interim benchmarks\nthe gains were negligible, and I don''t want to complicate this PR even\nfurther. It is a simple change which can be considered separately, see\nbelow\n\n<details>\n<summary>document fragment diff for future</summary>\n\n```diff\n       const merged = mergeNodes(preNodes, linkedSlice);\n-      for (const child of Array.from(merged.childNodes)) {\n-        state.pre.insertBefore(child, insertBeforeNode);\n+      // Move the merged nodes in one batch: a single insertion queues one\n+      // mutation record - instead of one per node - for any observer watching\n+      // the output (e.g. search highlighting).\n+      const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();\n+      while (merged.firstChild) {\n+        fragment.appendChild(merged.firstChild);\n       }\n+      state.pre.insertBefore(fragment, insertBeforeNode);\n     });\n```\n\n</details>\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone.\n\n## AI Usage\n\n- The initial code was hand-written in early 2025\n- In 2026 revival I:\n  - used Opus 4.8 to fix tests and a few minor regressions\n- iterated with Fable 5 (Max) to create benchmarks, further improve\nperformance and refactor the code\n  - used Opus 4.8 to assist with final checks\n  - used Copilot for self-review\n\n## Backports\n\nSome of the improvements are self-contained and could be backported:\n- [x] using `.includes()` over constructing a regex for fast-path\nsanitization heuristic\nhttps://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/pull/17262\n- [x] maybe the entire `replaceChangedNodes` logic? - no not doing so -\nattempted in https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/pull/17261 and it\ndid not seem worth it\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: pre-commit-ci[bot] <66853113+pre-commit-ci[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"e6a63e6d27474605707e5b6e984b3a6e86daeb01","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/trees/e6a63e6d27474605707e5b6e984b3a6e86daeb01"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/commits/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b","verification":{"payload":"tree e6a63e6d27474605707e5b6e984b3a6e86daeb01\nparent b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028\nauthor Michał Krassowski <5832902+krassowski@users.noreply.github.com> 1786122685 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786122685 +0100\n\nSpeed up large and streamed text outputs with incremental autolinking, preserve selection (#17197)\n\n## References\n\nFixes #16957\n\n## Code changes\n\nMakes the auto-linking logic execute asynchronously, at the speed\nmatching the approximate refresh rate of standard monitors. Appends new\nnodes in the textual streams without removing the existing ones\nminimising layout computation cost.\n\n1. **Spreads the work across frames**\n- Rendering happens in small steps inside a `requestAnimationFrame` loop\n(a browser callback that runs about once per screen refresh). Each frame\ndoes at most ~10 ms of auto-linking and then continues on the next\nframe, so one output cannot freeze the page.\n- Each step processes a block of text of about 8 KB (extended to the\nnext space), rather than one word or line at a time, which lowers the\nper-step overhead.\n- When several large outputs render at once, they take turns: only one\noutput is worked on per frame, and the loop cycles through them so each\nkeeps making visible progress and none stays blank.\n- A safety timer (named a \"watchdog\" in the code) still shows the plain\ntext if the browser stops sending animation frames, for example in a\nbackground tab or under heavy load. It only acts when frames have\ngenuinely stopped, not when an output is simply waiting for its turn.\n2. **Prioritises what the user can see**\n- Outputs scrolled off screen are found with an `IntersectionObserver`.\nWe use it instead of measuring visibility ourselves, because measuring\ndirectly would force the browser to recompute layout at a bad moment.\n- Off-screen outputs keep rendering, but quietly, during the browser''s\nidle time (`requestIdleCallback`, with a timer-based fallback for\nSafari). They are then ready when scrolled to, without slowing down the\noutputs on screen.\n- As soon as an off-screen output scrolls back into view, it returns to\nnormal per-frame rendering. The first paint of any output is never\ndelayed by visibility.\n3. **Updates the DOM without rebuilding it** (append-only, no trashing)\n   - Each output keeps a live `<pre>` element in two parts:\n- a \"committed\" part at the start: text that has already been linked and\nis never touched again;\n- a \"tail\" at the end: the not-yet-linked text, shown as plain text\nimmediately (with terminal colours kept), split into small pieces.\n- Every frame moves a little of the tail into the committed part after\nlinking it, so the amount of page update per frame depends only on how\nmuch was processed that frame, not on the total output size.\nFor streamed output, the next chunk reuses the page content from the\nprevious render instead of rebuilding it. Only the small region at the\nend that could still change is redone, for example a link split across\ntwo chunks (chunk one ends with `www.`, chunk two starts with\n`example.com`). This replaces the earlier approach that compared and\nswapped nodes.\n4. **Adds a logic to retain selection when updating text**. This is\nrequired because when we are replacing `www.example.com` in partially\nrendered text output `aaa www.example.com ccc` we are also implicitly\ndestroying the selection. This also leads to an improvement to status\nquo where selection was being destroyed on each stream chunk being\nadded.\n\n#### Notable edge cases\n\n- Search highlighting keeps working. Because the committed part is never\nrewritten, highlights added to it stay in place across frames. If\nanother feature changes the tail (for example search splitting or\nwrapping the text there), only the tail is rebuilt so committed\nhighlights are kept, and a full rebuild is used only as a last resort.\n- Text selection is preserved. When text is replaced during linking, the\nselection is remembered as character positions and restored afterwards.\nThis also fixes an existing problem where the selection was lost every\ntime a new chunk of streamed text arrived.\n- File-path links in error outputs (such as File \"example.py\", line 12)\nkeep their line number. Error outputs link both URLs and file paths, and\na file path can contain spaces, so steps there split on line breaks\ninstead of on spaces.\n- For very large outputs the slow part is the browser re-laying-out the\nshifted text on each frame, so the total time depends mostly on how many\nframes are used. In that case the per-frame budget is allowed to grow\n(up to 200 ms) so the work finishes in fewer frames, while small outputs\nkeep the responsive 10 ms budget. The budget only grows from this\noutput''s own previous frame, so several outputs rendering together do\nnot push each other''s budgets up.\n\n#### Code organisation\n\n- The live-page logic, node merging, and selection helpers are now\ncontained in the `LiveText` class in `rendermime/src/livetext.ts`.\n`renderers.ts` keeps the frame scheduling and the auto-linking.\n- A benchmark and analysis scripts were added under\n`galata/test/benchmark` to measure both stdout and stderr, streaming\nversus re-opening a notebook, and scaling with output size and number of\noutputs.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\n### It''s faster\n\nThe output content is displayed when it gets returned from the kernel as\nsoon as possible - no more choking the rendering pipeline due to too\nmuch output streamed at once.\n\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a8122a4e-4ea2-4ca4-ba35-53d5990b0611\n\nAs an example, when profiling the first paint of full (non-linkified)\ntext occurred in under 4 seconds (allowing me to read the last line),\nwhereas on 4.4.0a2 I had to wait 29 seconds to get it (but it was\nalready with all links displayed).\n\n### The links show up with a delay\n\nThe links are displayed as soon as these are processed. This means that\nusers may now see non-clickable links, but as seen on the recording\nabove, even though initially the text is not \"linkified\" the last links\nshows up faster than before! This is not guaranteed - it will depend on\nthe size of the output.\n\n> Note: the recording is from an earlier iteration; now links only show\nup with delay on much larger outputs; keeping that older recording as it\ndemonstrates the idea well; see benchmarks below for when it kicks in\nnowadays.\n\n### Selection is fun again\n\nSelection of outputs that is being streamed is not destroyed when new\noutput arrives\n\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/97741eb8-c6f0-4ba7-b11d-61253a617311\n\n### There is an opt-out\n\nI can''t think of a way this could introduce a regression, but still just\nin case if someone was depending on some quirk of the previous\nimplementation, this PR includes a setting to disable incremental\nrendering.\n\n<img width=\"975\" height=\"299\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/be35a0eb-a1bb-4626-89a2-9f00c410288e\"\n/>\n\nQuestion: should we use \"auto-link\" or \"autolink\"?\n\n### Latest benchmarks (comitted)\n\n### Table 1 Streaming on cell execution\n\n\n- incremental = this PR\n- synchronous = before/with incremental auto linking turned off\n\n\nDuring interactive execution both: full text, and all links show up much\nfaster than before. Note that in synchronous case all-text = all-links.\n\n\n#### Streaming stdout (web links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>1a - Streaming · stdout (web links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>85</td><td>79-91</td><td rowspan=\"3\">50</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">17</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>448</td><td>444-454</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>448</td><td>444-454</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>91</td><td>89-93</td><td rowspan=\"3\">117</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">100</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>1,819</td><td>1,814-1,830</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>1,819</td><td>1,814-1,830</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n\n#### Streaming stderr (web + path links)\n\n\n<table>\n  <caption>1b - Streaming · stderr (web + path links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>134</td><td>108-169</td><td rowspan=\"3\">55</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">22</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>452</td><td>414-476</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>452</td><td>414-476</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>284</td><td>246-318</td><td rowspan=\"3\">183</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">117</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>12,283</td><td>12,132-12,409</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>12,283</td><td>12,132-12,409</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n\n### Table 2 Rendering on re-opening a notebook\n\n#### Bulk rendering stdout (web links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>2a - Re-open · stdout (web links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td><td rowspan=\"3\">72</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">17</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>310</td><td>299-323</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td><td rowspan=\"3\">90</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">28</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>298</td><td>296-303</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n#### Bulk rendering on re-open · stderr (web + path links)\n\n<table>\n  <caption>2b - Re-open · stderr (web + path links)</caption>\n  <thead>\n<tr><th>pipeline</th><th>time\nto</th><th>IQM</th><th>Q1-Q3</th><th>longest frame</th><th>median\nframe</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">incremental</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td><td rowspan=\"3\">95</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">22</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>348</td><td>343-360</td></tr>\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\" scope=\"rowgroup\">synchronous</th><td>first\ntext</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td><td rowspan=\"3\">95</td><td\nrowspan=\"3\">33</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all text</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>all links</td><td>339</td><td>334-344</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\nNote that for synchronous scenario all values are identical on reopening\nhere as expected.\n\n#### Scaling with output size - streaming stderr\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1772a31e-55d0-421b-afab-f68be7baf759\"\n/>\n\n(1600 blocks synchronous pipeline did not run until completion as it\nexceeded 5 minutes threshold)\n\n#### Scaling with output size - re-opening a notebook with stdout\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/66fbcb83-f655-4e01-8958-a057b7786a40\"\n/>\n\n#### Scaling with number of outputs - re-opening a notebook with stderr\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/470b1ea5-87c0-4721-99b8-adeb3c179ecf\"\n/>\n\n#### Linkification only starts to lag behind text when re-opening a\nnotebook with very large output\n\n<img width=\"1800\" height=\"680\" alt=\"image\"\nsrc=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9b6c5dff-82d0-42a8-9150-98a65860c40e\"\n/>\n\n\n\n## Future work\n\nPotential improvements:\n- We could try to infer the refresh rate of the monitor using intervals\nbetween `requestAnimationFrame` and use it instead of a hard-coded time\nlimit; this might improve the experience for users with high-refresh\nrate monitors (bot only if they are also using high-end CPU/GPU\ncombination that can process enough `linkify` passes in such shorter\nduration of the frame to avoid frame dropping)\n- We could avoid the layout cost by imposing size restriction on the\noutput if no new text was streamed (but it is only being auto-linked).\nThis is because the links should be rendered using the same font (but\nonly different colour).\n- I attempted it but in initial attempts I did not see significant gains\n- Instead of constantly calling `requestAnimationFrame` (and cancelling\nit) we could call it once and update arguments if new chunk is streamed;\nthis is because there is an overhead associated (approx 0.6s for each\n10s so not terrible, but noticeable); instead we could pass state via a\n`WeakMap` or similar.\n\n\n\n### Already handled in this PR (were previously listed here)\n\n- Re-layout cost: with the append-only update, committed text is never\nlaid out again. Together with `contain: style layout` this gives a very\ngood performance; I previously explored also fixing the size but it is\nnot necessary nor desirable\n- Frame timing on capable machines: handled by letting the per-frame\nbudget grow, without having to detect the exact monitor refresh rate.\n\n### To consider in future PRs\n\n- **Parsing repeats over the whole output on every chunk**: Each\nstreamed chunk re-cleans and re-parses all the text received so far, not\nonly the new part. Now that updating the page is cheap per chunk, this\nparsing is the main remaining cost for long streams. Parsing only the\nnew text (while carrying terminal-colour state from the previous chunk)\nwould fix it.\n- **High-refresh-rate monitors**: We could measure the time between\nframes and stop assuming 60 frames per second, but this only helps where\nthe CPU and GPU can also fit enough linking work into the shorter frame.\nI did attempt that but it did not seem worth it at the time.\n- **Animation-frame scheduling overhead**: The loop requests and cancels\na frame on every step (a small but measurable cost). Requesting a frame\nonce and passing state through a `WeakMap` would remove it (at a cost of\nmaking it harder to reason about).\n- **Progress bars that rewrite the same line** (using `\\r`, like tqdm):\nThese still redo work on each update. This is acceptable for now because\nwe do not expect outputs which are 10s of MBs in size to be constrained\nto a single line (which would be somewhat pathological, even for random\nbytes to not include a single `\\n`), but the impact on when it does\nhappen could be limited with more work.\n- **One `IntersectionObserver` is created per output**: A single shared\nobserver would be lighter for notebooks with thousands of outputs (but\npotentially make the code more complex to reason about).\n- **Using document fragments for one-pass insertion**: This could reduce\nthe number of DOM insertions (but make it larger); in interim benchmarks\nthe gains were negligible, and I don''t want to complicate this PR even\nfurther. It is a simple change which can be considered separately, see\nbelow\n\n<details>\n<summary>document fragment diff for future</summary>\n\n```diff\n       const merged = mergeNodes(preNodes, linkedSlice);\n-      for (const child of Array.from(merged.childNodes)) {\n-        state.pre.insertBefore(child, insertBeforeNode);\n+      // Move the merged nodes in one batch: a single insertion queues one\n+      // mutation record - instead of one per node - for any observer watching\n+      // the output (e.g. search highlighting).\n+      const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();\n+      while (merged.firstChild) {\n+        fragment.appendChild(merged.firstChild);\n       }\n+      state.pre.insertBefore(fragment, insertBeforeNode);\n     });\n```\n\n</details>\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone.\n\n## AI Usage\n\n- The initial code was hand-written in early 2025\n- In 2026 revival I:\n  - used Opus 4.8 to fix tests and a few minor regressions\n- iterated with Fable 5 (Max) to create benchmarks, further improve\nperformance and refactor the code\n  - used Opus 4.8 to assist with final checks\n  - used Copilot for self-review\n\n## Backports\n\nSome of the improvements are self-contained and could be backported:\n- [x] using `.includes()` over constructing a regex for fast-path\nsanitization heuristic\nhttps://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/pull/17262\n- [x] maybe the entire `replaceChangedNodes` logic? - no not doing so -\nattempted in https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/pull/17261 and it\ndid not seem worth it\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: pre-commit-ci[bot] <66853113+pre-commit-ci[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqdhG9CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAcu0QAB9+hQWquUxNJYK+FB1z/Fi7\nYLjtnU36YV7p/WggopGsedJVPYbmMtnGh+v1HlcxoFljYJ3Ya+Auvm1vqHvoPf23\n1rDELql5iTcujYr7PIfOPw0dloBSDkSqEaXhQ5z1YNNTEv0OmhLO7U8OEMP2VNvl\n9cPFIOP60dppRmVTeAnfEpOLx2c/z4EDgew+WJBvnvC+99YQjzQMXcC42/Jv7eWH\n+59dfDssIuOwID+jlm4ZR68u1VohBPKLTpCeekBUcB9lOxFj1egSbevoA6VsUkhP\njCvjCl/PcSVQuR3V/T3541tubS1dN/YZbXr5LNQzthB/rNqovqmxiaS+W68ySng6\nmy2Km4+J93PoIx2RcEB60nT3na+1Z2pRjfCzH+whJ8CbApefLvrFvu0uLPP+FgQx\nZXDSZ2ev0xY729xBtD4r4YcKiXJrIL2hxziXLH9NbadahKf1XCFsp1+BTVgBarUE\nMLArQlqk5iqug0zXZn5Ij5Hgj1Y4Y2eMCa6hOjoi6n1B0IdeeTqXhEE1dQURtUTG\nLA48QOSs1IuOF1QhYcNa9nZguITcVKGv6CS3pUCZO1wfi4UXrf5bPfXDsOCay8aS\nN/ddRtnPcCpeNG7fWiFNpxpOY41u3unfm6kWdmiYDYM37gIMhaCiDDJpUIH7rn5q\nHtvWSjeAKDCS20GoGmE9\n=i2wa\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-07T17:11:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commit/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b","node_id":"C_kwDOA5k_I9oAKDdiN2MyY2RlNTljNmU5YzUxMDYyM2MyNDUwNDE4ZTM0OTIzYzI2NWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commit/b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028","sha":"b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028"}],"sha":"7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/7b7c2cde59c6e9c510623c2450418e34923c265b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/127468609?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Darshan808","id":127468609,"login":"Darshan808","node_id":"U_kgDOB5kEQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Darshan808","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/commits/b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-07T13:18:00Z","email":"pranishpoudel10@gmail.com","name":"Darshan Poudel"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-07T13:18:00Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":" Add flaky-test guardrails to Galata tests via `eslint-plugin-playwright` (#19106)\n\n## References #19002\n\n## Description\n\nAdopts `eslint-plugin-playwright` and enables five rules that catch the\nmost common sources of flaky end-to-end tests. The rules are scoped to\nthe Galata test suite (`galata/test/**`) and enforced at `error`.\n\n| Rule | Catches | Preferred alternative |\n|------|----------|-----------------------|\n| `no-wait-for-timeout` | `page.waitForTimeout(…)` |\n`Utils.waitForCondition()` or a web-first assertion |\n| `no-element-handle` | `$`, `$$`, `elementHandle()`, `elementHandles()`\n| Locators (auto-retry on DOM updates) |\n| `no-networkidle` | the discouraged `networkidle` load state | wait on\na specific condition |\n| `prefer-to-have-count` | `expect(await locator.count()).toBe(n)` |\n`expect(locator).toHaveCount(n)` |\n| `prefer-web-first-assertions` | `expect(await\nlocator.isVisible()/textContent()/…).toBe(…)` | `toBeVisible()`,\n`toHaveText()`, etc. |\n\n\nEnabling the rules surfaces 118 existing violations across 36 test files\n(61 `no-wait-for-timeout`, 41 `prefer-web-first-assertions`, 12\n`prefer-to-have-count`, and 2 each for `no-networkidle` and\n`no-element-handle`). These are pre-existing issues rather than\nregressions..\n\nAdds `eslint-plugin-playwright@^2.2.0` as a dependency.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nNone.\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone.\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **Yes**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES).\n- AI tools and models used: Claude Code","tree":{"sha":"cca3c457fd2c8413c55ec09ba15f48614c127761","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/trees/cca3c457fd2c8413c55ec09ba15f48614c127761"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/git/commits/b13fdf4f052215044ebcbc5dae94e8562a59a028","verification":{"payload":"tree cca3c457fd2c8413c55ec09ba15f48614c127761\nparent c92f5efa1285d4e091f8a73a6a661439ca347670\nauthor Darshan Poudel <pranishpoudel10@gmail.com> 1786108680 +0545\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786108680 +0100\n\n Add flaky-test guardrails to Galata tests via `eslint-plugin-playwright` (#19106)\n\n## References #19002\n\n## Description\n\nAdopts `eslint-plugin-playwright` and enables five rules that catch the\nmost common sources of flaky end-to-end tests. The rules are scoped to\nthe Galata test suite (`galata/test/**`) and enforced at `error`.\n\n| Rule | Catches | Preferred alternative |\n|------|----------|-----------------------|\n| `no-wait-for-timeout` | `page.waitForTimeout(…)` |\n`Utils.waitForCondition()` or a web-first assertion |\n| `no-element-handle` | `$`, `$$`, `elementHandle()`, `elementHandles()`\n| Locators (auto-retry on DOM updates) |\n| `no-networkidle` | the discouraged `networkidle` load state | wait on\na specific condition |\n| `prefer-to-have-count` | `expect(await locator.count()).toBe(n)` |\n`expect(locator).toHaveCount(n)` |\n| `prefer-web-first-assertions` | `expect(await\nlocator.isVisible()/textContent()/…).toBe(…)` | `toBeVisible()`,\n`toHaveText()`, etc. |\n\n\nEnabling the rules surfaces 118 existing violations across 36 test files\n(61 `no-wait-for-timeout`, 41 `prefer-web-first-assertions`, 12\n`prefer-to-have-count`, and 2 each for `no-networkidle` and\n`no-element-handle`). These are pre-existing issues rather than\nregressions..\n\nAdds `eslint-plugin-playwright@^2.2.0` as a dependency.\n\n## User-facing changes\n\nNone.\n\n## Backwards-incompatible changes\n\nNone.\n\n## AI usage\n\n- **Yes**: Some or all of the content of this PR was generated by AI.\n- **YES**: The human author has carefully reviewed this PR and run this\ncode (keep this PR \"draft\" until the answer is YES).\n- AI tools and models used: Claude Code","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqddsICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANHQQAGzswF4142kd0CQU91daLCNr\nQHq7jziy1mresxgg79hU4+MdjOkdNqAXUX5oaG0vpOgJKHf65kD5esAVPJhcQDUd\npydzw+nfAtsBN0/guLXPYouROs6UVF4PFnMBUrpiuTGxJVIrakZjVV5X3ch5Xhi2\n6JPTXulZrZJbekEHaX03ksqJhoV47y9kog5xcdXKvgwGrB4uXDnltX+SETdIaaY1\nRx1zKO5rfWonpE//7zep/1NCDBvNxw+Ezdbl9pyX5b7DhzO3fTHepT1qBP9u9ECu\nD4IVZ72zkicXBIXBqUYBJPPFI2l0RlrwjJSyGp6Z28OAlfxNEjtSRQV9eOZZkP